# Flutter Chat App - Workspace Guidelines

## Project Overview
Transform Flutter chat app into world-class messaging platform benchmarked against WhatsApp, Messenger, Zalo, Telegram standards.

## Performance Targets
- App startup time: <2 seconds
- Message delivery: <100ms
- Memory usage: <150MB average
- Support: 10,000+ messages per conversation
- Crash rate: <0.1%
- Test coverage: >90%

## Architecture Standards

### Clean Architecture Implementation
- Domain layer: Pure business logic, no external dependencies
- Data layer: Repository implementations, data sources, models with proper API mapping
- Presentation layer: UI components, BLoCs, widgets with Material Design 3
- Dependency flow: Presentation → Domain ← Data

### State Management with BLoC
- Use BLoC pattern for all complex state management
- Implement proper event/state separation with freezed
- Handle loading, success, error states consistently
- Use Cubit for simple local state management
- Implement proper subscription disposal in BLoCs

### API Integration Requirements
- **CRITICAL**: Always sync Flutter models with NestJS backend entities
- Map GraphQL schema types correctly to Dart models
- Handle OfficeChatConversation → ChatModel mapping properly
- Ensure OfficeChatMessage → MessageModel consistency
- Implement proper error handling for API failures
- Use proper authentication with JWT token management

## Data Models Synchronization

### User Model Mapping
```dart
// Flutter: UserModel ↔ Backend: OfficeUser
- serverId ↔ id (UUID)
- username ↔ username
- displayName ↔ fullname
- avatarUrl ↔ imageUrls[0]
- isOnline ↔ status (Active/Inactive)
- lastSeen ↔ updatedAt
```

### Chat Model Mapping
```dart
// Flutter: ChatModel ↔ Backend: OfficeChatConversation
- serverId ↔ id (UUID)
- name ↔ name
- type ↔ type (Direct/Group)
- participantIds ↔ members[].userId
- createdAt ↔ createdAt
- lastMessageAt ↔ lastMessageAt
```

### Message Model Mapping
```dart
// Flutter: MessageModel ↔ Backend: OfficeChatMessage
- localId ↔ id
- chatId ↔ conversationId
- senderId ↔ senderId
- content ↔ message
- type ↔ type (Text/Image/File)
- createdAt ↔ createdAt (timestamp)
- readBy ↔ readerIds
```

## Code Quality Standards
- Use freezed for all data classes and sealed unions
- Implement proper equals/hashCode with Equatable
- Use const constructors wherever possible
- Follow naming conventions: classes PascalCase, methods camelCase
- Add comprehensive documentation for public APIs
- Implement proper error handling with custom exceptions

## Testing Strategy
- Unit tests: 95% coverage for domain layer
- Widget tests: 85% coverage for presentation layer
- Integration tests: 80% coverage for critical user flows
- Use bloc_test for BLoC testing
- Mock all external dependencies properly
- Test error scenarios and edge cases

## Performance Optimization
- Use virtual scrolling for message lists
- Implement efficient image caching with proper compression
- Use isolates for heavy operations (encryption, file processing)
- Optimize network requests with proper batching
- Implement connection pooling for WebSocket management
- Monitor memory usage and implement proper disposal patterns

## Security Requirements
- Implement input validation and sanitization
- Use secure storage for sensitive data
- Add proper authentication and authorization
- Implement rate limiting for API calls
- Add logging with structured data for debugging
- Use HTTPS for all network communications

## Development Workflow
- Follow Git flow with feature branches
- Require code review for all changes
- Run automated tests before merging
- Use semantic versioning for releases
- Document all architectural decisions
- Maintain changelog for all releases
